# Development Guide

This document describes the development process and code standards for the fBit project showcase (`fbit-dev`), a Next.js site that presents fBit projects.

## Development Setup

### Prerequisites

- Node.js 20.9 or newer and npm
- Git

### Initial Setup

1. **Clone the repository:**
```bash
git clone https://github.com/fbit-solutions/fbit-dev.git
cd fbit-dev
```

2. **Install dependencies:**
```bash
npm install
```

3. **Start the dev server:**
```bash
npm run dev
```

Open http://localhost:3000. The site has no database, authentication or required environment variables.

## Project Structure

| Path | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `content/projects.ts` | All project data (name, description, status, tags, tech, links) |
| `components/` | UI components, e.g. the project browser (filters, search, cards) |
| `app/` | App Router pages, layout and global styles (`globals.css`) |
| `lib/` | Shared helpers |
| `public/brand/` | fBit logos and avatar |
| `public/projects/` | Project screenshots |

## Development Workflow

### Branching Strategy

- **main** - Production-ready code
- **feature/\*** - New features, branched from `main`
- **fix/\*** - Bug fixes, branched from `main`
- **docs/\*** - Documentation updates

### Making Changes

1. Create a branch from `main`:
```bash
git checkout main
git pull origin main
git checkout -b feature/your-feature-name
```

2. Make your changes and commit:
```bash
git add .
git commit -m "feat: description of changes"
```

3. Push and open a Pull Request to `main`:
```bash
git push origin feature/your-feature-name
```

### Commit Message Format

Follow conventional commits:
- `feat:` - New feature
- `fix:` - Bug fix
- `docs:` - Documentation
- `style:` - Formatting or styling only
- `refactor:` - Code refactoring
- `perf:` - Performance improvements
- `chore:` - Build tasks, dependencies, etc.

Example: `feat: add technology chips to project cards`

## Code Standards

- **Language:** TypeScript
- **Framework:** Next.js 15 with the App Router and React 19
- **Styling:** Custom CSS in `app/globals.css` (no Tailwind)
- **Icons:** `lucide-react`

### Adding or Updating a Project

Edit `content/projects.ts`. Every project needs:

- `name`, `slug` (unique), `category`, `description`
- `status` - one of `Live`, `In development`, `Alpha Testing`, `Beta Testing`, `Private Preview`, `Details coming soon`
- `tags` and `tech` (the "Built with" chips)
- `href` (optional) - live site or GitHub repository

If you add a new `status` value, add a matching style in `globals.css`, because status classes are derived from the lowercased status with spaces replaced by `-`.

Keep descriptions factual. Do not claim features that are not confirmed.

## Testing

There is no automated test suite or linter. Before opening a PR:

- [ ] `npm run build` succeeds (it also type-checks)
- [ ] No console errors or warnings in `npm run dev`
- [ ] Category filters and search work
- [ ] Mobile and desktop layouts verified
- [ ] External links open the correct URL

```bash
# Type-check and build the production bundle
npm run build

# Serve the production build locally
npm run start
```

## Deployment

Build the site with `npm run build` and host it on any Next.js-capable platform. Configure the production domain in `app/layout.tsx` (`metadataBase`) before publishing.

## Troubleshooting

### Build Fails

1. Check the Node version: `node --version` (should be 20.9+)
2. Clear the cache and reinstall:
```bash
rm -rf .next node_modules && npm install
```
3. Check the `npm run build` output for type errors

### Styling Looks Broken

1. Stop the dev server, delete `.next`, and start it again
2. Check `app/globals.css` for unbalanced braces after edits
3. Verify that a new `status` value has a matching CSS rule

## Performance

- Use the Next.js `Image` component for images
- Optimise screenshots before adding them to `public/projects/`

## Security

- Never commit `.env*` files (ignored in `.gitignore`)
- Do not publish private repository URLs or credentials in `content/projects.ts`
- Add a privacy page before adding analytics or forms that collect personal data

